{{define "determinism_demands"}}An acceptance criterion that demands that model behaviour holds in every run — "100%", "always", "never", "every run", or one passing run offered as proof — asks for what a model cannot give, and pushes the implementer toward rigid code. Report it as `category: ambiguous_spec`, `criterion: determinism_demand`, `severity: major`, quoting the criterion in `evidence`. In `suggestion`, either rewrite it as a rate — a named eval, n of at least 10, a threshold and a baseline — or, when it guards a commitment code can check from structured data (a consent stamp, a submission, eligibility, verbatim legal text), move it to a deterministic code check, where 100% is correct.

Not a determinism demand: a safety invariant checked deterministically from structured state; verbatim legal text; any criterion of a build task about code output or structured state. An agent-network plan also has deterministic tasks — mappers, an opt-out keyword matcher, harness code — and "never" and "100%" are right there.
{{end}}{{define "experiment_protocol"}}An experiment is a change to model behaviour that is measured, then kept or reverted. Its acceptance criteria must state the protocol, not a guaranteed outcome: the target eval, n, the baseline rate, the threshold, and the keep-or-revert rule. Report each of these as `category: missing_acceptance_criterion`, `criterion: experiment_protocol`, `severity: major`:

- a piece of the protocol that is missing;
- n below 10, or below a higher minimum the plan states;
- a keep rule that does not depend on both the after-rate and the regression-suite result — "keep regardless" is not a rule;
- a threshold at or inside the baseline's stated interval, so that noise alone could meet it.

A revert that follows the stated rule is a valid outcome: do not ask the criteria to guarantee that the target is met. The server checks the task's rung; do not report a missing one.
{{end}}{{define "fix_ladder"}}### Fix ladder

This plan is agent-network work. A task's `**Rung:**` header names the fix-ladder rung it works at, lowest first: `oracle` (the eval or judge is wrong), `variance` (run-to-run noise), `facts` (the facts and ids the model is shown), `tool` (the tools it can call), `commitment` (a code gate over structured data), `prompt`, and `owner` (a decision only the product owner can make). A fix belongs on the lowest rung that holds.

- A task at rung `commitment` whose `Context:` gives no evidence that the lower rungs — oracle, variance, facts, tool — were ruled out: report `category: boundary_violation`, `criterion: fix_ladder`, `severity: major`, {{.}}.
- When every fix task in the plan is at rung `commitment`, and none is at `facts` or `prompt`, report it once as a plan-level finding with the same category, criterion and severity.

Only `commitment` is checked: a code gate is the rung that makes the network rigid, so it is the one that must show the lower rungs were tried. Do not report a task at another rung for skipping rungs.

{{end}}{{define "rates_as_evidence"}}### Rates as evidence

This task is agent-network work, so model behaviour is judged as a rate, not by one run.

- A rate report — k/n per eval, with intervals — is evidence. Judge a behaviour criterion against its stated threshold. A failed sample inside a rate that meets the threshold is not a `correctness` or `test_adequacy` defect.
- For a criterion stated as a rate, Test adequacy above is replaced by four checks: the eval has frozen cases that exercise the behaviour; n, the baseline and the threshold are stated; the regression suite ran; and no `pinned_by` suite shows a rate outside its recorded baseline interval. Report a failed check as `test_adequacy`.
- One run offered as the evidence for a behaviour criterion is `insufficient_evidence`, `severity: minor`.

{{end}}{{define "experiment_outcome"}}### Experiment outcome

This task is an experiment, so grade its protocol, not its target:

- the rate was measured before and after, at the stated n;
- the regression suite ran;
- the keep-or-revert rule was applied as stated;
- the result was recorded.

A revert that follows the stated rule meets the acceptance criteria, with or without a diff. A kept change whose numbers do not meet the stated rule is `missing_acceptance_criterion`, `severity: major`. A missing measurement is `insufficient_evidence`. A result recorded as kept — in the rate digest or in the summary — with no diff is `insufficient_evidence`, `severity: major`: a kept change must be shown, so that the boundary check can run on it.

{{end}}
